# DTF tier manifest: signing-ed25519-rotation-overlap-legacy
#   1.11.0 OpenPGP signing-key features and fixes for Debian/RPM metadata:
#   #1326 Ed25519 OpenPGP keys (and key_type=ed25519 refused with a pointer at
#   the gpg form), #1329 the rotation overlap window (dual signatures, both
#   keys served, revoke ends it, =0 disables it), #1331 startup migration of
#   legacy keys with SIGNING_AUTO_MIGRATE_LEGACY_KEYS, #4416 rotating an
#   unscoped key repoints the repositories bound to it.
#   RED ak-backend:1.10.0, GREEN ak-backend:1.11.0 and later.
#
# Profile-set: filesystem + client.apt (debian:12, the real apt-secure
# verifier). gpg/gpgv on the host running the oracle (as the signing tier):
# a hard preflight, not a skip. The oracle keeps its own GNUPGHOME.
#
# TWO BOOTS on the same compose project (the xff-authz-trust idiom):
#   phase 1  default env: SIGNING_KEY_ROTATION_OVERLAP_SECS unset (14 days).
#   phase 2  only the backend recreated with SIGNING_AUTO_MIGRATE_LEGACY_KEYS=true
#            and SIGNING_KEY_ROTATION_OVERLAP_SECS=0 (json-file logging, so
#            the startup migration line is readable with docker logs).
#
# THE PRIMITIVES
#   Public Debian/RPM repositories (the apt client needs no credentials).
#   Keys bound with POST /api/v1/signing/repositories/{id}/config. Signature
#   packets counted with `gpg --list-packets` on the detached signature or the
#   clearsigned block; verification with gpgv against the served keyring.
#   apt-get update inside client-apt with signed-by= one key file and no
#   [trusted=yes]; apt exits 0 on a refused InRelease ("previous index files
#   will be used"), so the oracle reads its E:/W: lines too.
#   The legacy binding (#1331): the API refuses an rsa key on Debian metadata,
#   so a gpg binding is made through the API and its signing_key_id is set to
#   a key_type=rsa key with SQL (InRelease is then 500 on every image).
#
# THE GATES
#   (CONTROL)  both images: an unscoped gpg rsa2048 key bound to a Debian and
#              an RPM repository; InRelease and repomd.xml.asc verify with
#              gpgv; apt trusting it updates with no error line.
#   (FIX #1326) key_type=ed25519 -> 400 whose message names key_type='gpg'
#              with algorithm='ed25519'; gpg + ed25519 creates a key with a
#              40-hex fingerprint whose served public key is algo 22 (EdDSA);
#              InRelease and Release.gpg are one algo-22 / digest-10 (SHA-512)
#              signature each and gpgv verifies both.
#   (FIX #4416/#1329) rotate: both bindings point at the successor; InRelease,
#              Release.gpg and repomd.xml.asc carry 2 signature packets incl.
#              the old keyid; gpg-key.asc and repomd.xml.key serve 2 keys;
#              old key inactive with expires_at = now + 1209600 s (+-120 s);
#              the signing_key_audit 'rotated' row has overlap_ends_at and
#              both repointed repository ids.
#   (FIX #1329) apt trusting only the OLD key updates cleanly during the
#              overlap; after revoke: expires_at <= now, one signature (not
#              the old key), and the same client gets a NO_PUBKEY GPG error.
#   (FIX #1331) after the phase-2 boot the legacy repository is bound to a new
#              key_type=gpg key, the old key has an 'auto_migrated' audit row
#              naming it, InRelease is 200 and verifies, and the startup log
#              has the SIGNING_AUTO_MIGRATE_LEGACY_KEYS line.
#   (FIX #1329) with SIGNING_KEY_ROTATION_OVERLAP_SECS=0 a rotation leaves one
#              signature and one served key, the old key expires at the
#              rotation time and the audit's overlap_ends_at is null.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~70 authenticated calls back to back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem client.apt"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
